+## Subtopics
 
-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Boundary Pixels:** Pixels on the edge lack neighbors on one or more sides [S1, unofficial]. The implementation checks bounds explicitly (`if (param.X - 1 >= 0 && param.X + 1 < param.GrayscaleImage.Width && param.Y - 1 >= 0 && param.Y + 1 < param.GrayscaleImage.Height)`) [S1, unofficial]. If out of bounds, it defaults to adding voxels from 0 to the computed height [S1, unofficial].
-- **Negative/Zero Height:** When excavating, if the computed height is less than or equal to the neighbor minimum, the algorithm places a single voxel at `max(0, height - 1)` instead of an empty column or negative height [S1, unofficial].
-- **Precision:** The formula uses the constant 65535 for normalization [S1, unofficial]. Implementers must verify the input image's bit-depth matches this scaling factor, as 8-bit images (0-255) will yield near-zero heights unless `heightFactor` is adjusted or the denominator is modified [S1, unofficial].
+### [Height Curve Activation & Voxel Column Generation](17940-height-curve-activation-voxel-column-generation.md)
+This method transforms a normalized 2D noise heightmap into a 3D voxel column structure by applying a hyperbolic tangent activation function to smooth terrain features, clamping values to a water line, and stacking voxels from a defined ground offset to the calculated height. It differs from simple linear extrusion by using a non-linear activation curve to compress extreme noise values, providing more natural-looking terrain profiles, and explicitly handles color mapping based on discrete terrain bands.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$, where $W$ and $H$ are image dimensions [G]. Neighbor lookups are constant-time (4 neighbors), preserving linear scaling [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$ for the output voxel list [G]. Maximum voxel count scales with $W \times H \times H_{max}$ [G].
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement; generates terrain-like structures efficiently; "Excavate" mode reduces vertical artifacts and creates smoother transitions [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Resolution is strictly bound to the input image dimensions; height resolution depends on `heightFactor` and channel precision; boundary pixels may exhibit uneven edges due to missing neighbor data [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** Converting 2D heightmaps or grayscale images into 3D voxel data for games or simulations where performance is critical and high-fidelity surface detail is not required [S1, unofficial].
-- **When not to use:** When high-resolution surface geometry is needed (use mesh extraction instead); when the input image is not a heightmap (e.g., color textures should be mapped differently) [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$, where $W$ and $H$ are the image dimensions. Each pixel is processed once, and neighbor checks are constant time (4 neighbors).
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$ to store the intermediate height map if precomputed, or $O(V)$ for the output voxel list, where $V$ is the number of voxels.
+- **Strengths:** Simple to implement; efficient for generating terrain-like structures from heightmaps; excavate mode prevents floating islands.
+- **Limitations:** Resolution is limited by image resolution; no sub-voxel precision; excavate mode can flatten steep features.
+- **When to use:** When converting 2D heightmaps (e.g., from noise functions or images) into 3D voxel data for games or simulations.
+- **When not to use:** When high-resolution smooth surfaces are required (use mesh-based approaches) or when complex 3D geometry is needed beyond heightfields.
 
 ## Background
-This method is implemented in the **FileToVox** tool, an unofficial utility for converting various file formats (including QB files and images) into Minecraft-style `.vox` files [S1, unofficial]. The specific "Excavate" mode and heightmap generation logic are part of its image processing pipeline [S1, unofficial].
+This method is part of the voxelization pipeline in tools like FileToVox, which converts various file formats into voxel data. It is commonly used in Minecraft modding, voxel-based games, and procedural terrain generation. The 'excavate' mode is a specific heuristic to maintain structural integrity in voxel art.
 
